:: F2 {"position":"525,25","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: c) Lactate fermentation

When oxygen availability falls, glycolysis can continue if NAD+ is regenerated through lactate fermentation. Pyruvate is reduced to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase, allowing continued ATP production from glycolysis. Glycogen synthesis stores glucose rather than generating ATP, gluconeogenesis produces glucose, and ketogenesis occurs mainly in the liver. This adaptation supports short-term energy production during intense muscular activity.

