:: F3 {"position":"775,25","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: a) At a site distinct from the active site

Allosteric regulators bind to a regulatory site separate from the enzyme active site. This interaction changes enzyme conformation and alters catalytic activity, providing rapid control of metabolic flux. Binding is not necessarily irreversible. Allosteric regulators generally act on proteins rather than exclusively on DNA, and they do not inherently bind ATP. Allosteric control is a major mechanism for coordinating pathway activity with cellular demand.

