:: F5 {"position":"25,150","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: b) Phosphorylation

Phosphorylation involves addition of a phosphate group by protein kinases and is one of the most important mechanisms regulating enzyme activity. The modification can activate or inhibit target proteins depending on context. Ubiquitination often marks proteins for degradation, glycosylation adds carbohydrate groups, and replication is DNA duplication. Reversible phosphorylation enables rapid regulation of metabolism in response to hormones and nutrient status.

